# sky130A — open reference extraction deck for vyges-extract
#
# Capacitance is calibrated against the foundry-reference extractor (OpenROAD **OpenRCX**, nom
# corner, rules.openrcx.sky130A.nom) across a **set of 10 sky130 blocks**, each scored against
# the SPEF its own OpenLane run produced: CF_UART_APB, counter, fft_top, humidistat_apb,
# mag_phase_apb, rv_plic_lite, spectrum_buffer, spi_host_lite, temp_sensor_apb, xbar_main.
#
# `fft_ctrl_tlul` (14 238 nets) was **held out of every fit** and scored only afterwards:
#
#     ground 0.98x   coupling 1.02x   TOTAL 1.00x
#     per-net median: ground 0.95 (p10 0.73, p90 1.20), coupling 1.04 (p10 0.83, p90 1.24)
#
# Fitted by `correlation/calibrate_coupling.py` then `correlation/calibrate_ground.py`, in that
# order — `shield_k` multiplies OUR coupling, so it cannot be fitted until the coupling column is
# trustworthy, or you are fitting one error against another. See correlation/ground-vs-coupling.md.
#
# ── shield_k — why ground is not just a per-um number ──────────────────────────────────
# Field that terminates on a neighbour is field that did not terminate on ground, so a net's
# grounded cap is reduced by `shield_k * Cc_net`. Without it a deck fitted on a sparse block
# over-states ground on a dense one — the previous deck tracked `counter` to 0.997 and ran 1.37x
# over on a real block, with a +0.94 correlation between a net's coupling-to-ground ratio and its
# error. `Cc_net` here counts a net's coupling from BOTH sides, matching `engine.rs`; expressed
# against coupling counted once the same fraction reads ~0.36.
#
# ── Known bound: no interlayer term ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This deck defines no `interlayer` coefficients, so crossover (layer-to-layer) coupling is
# computed as ZERO and the lateral coefficients absorb it. The tell: at minimum spacing the
# fitted met1 coupling is ~2.5x the ideal sidewall parallel-plate value, which it cannot exceed
# on physics. The fit reproduces OpenRCX per-net while being wrong per-mechanism. It holds while
# the crossover-to-lateral ratio resembles these blocks — all sky130 std-cell digital — and
# should NOT be trusted where layer usage differs markedly. Adding interlayer terms and
# re-fitting lateral and crossover jointly is the next depth step.
#
# ── Unconstrained layers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# li1 is UNFITTED (0 constraining nets): sky130 routing uses li1 only for via landings, so no
# li1 wire segment appears anywhere in the set. met4/met5 carry met3's fitted values (187 and 0
# nets). A least-squares fit returns 0.0 for an unconstrained layer, which would silently switch
# that layer off; both scripts refuse coefficients under 200 nets and carry a stated placeholder.
#
# ── Resistance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Physical, from the sky130_fd_sc_hd nom tech LEF (RESISTANCE RPERSQ). Not calibrated against
# anything; an R correlation pass is separate.
#
# Calibrated to OpenRCX, a reference extractor — NOT to silicon. A sign-off / certified per-fab
# deck is silicon-correlated and NDA, and is never in this repo.
#
# layer  res(ohm/um)  cap(fF/um)  coupling(fF/um)  s_ref(um)
li1      12.8         0.060        0.030            0.17
met1      0.125       0.12494      0.21941          0.14
met2      0.125       0.09676      0.26618          0.14
met3      0.047       0.13365      0.26458          0.30
met4      0.047       0.13365      0.26458          0.30
met5      0.0285      0.13365      0.26458          1.60
via       9.3
couple_cutoff 2.0     # um — ignore lateral coupling beyond this gap
shield_k  0.18081     # grounded cap -= shield_k * Cc_net (both sides) — see above
